{"type":"thinking","text":"The nametable read used the wrong nametable/base mapping — it returned tile 36 everywhere, meaning the read of nametable 1 at those coords doesn't reflect the screen (maybe because the screen is drawn from nametable 1 but with a different base or the game decompresses). Actually get_tile_at_pixel at (44,88) gave nametable 1, tile_x 5, tile_y 11. read_nametable(1, 5,11) returned tile 36. That's inconsistent! get_tile_at_pixel gave tile_id 86 at tile_x=5,tile_y=11, but read_nametable says 36.\n\nHmm. Possibly read_nametable reads the raw nametable before the game's VRAM writes? Or maybe there's a mistake: get_tile_at_pixel's tile_x=5 might be the screen tile column, not nametable column. Actually screen_box x=44 → tile column 5 (44/8=5.5 → 5). And it says tile_x:5. And tile_y=11 (88/8=11). So tile_x/tile_y are screen tile coords, not nametable coords! The nametable column would be (scroll_x + screen x)/8. With scroll_x=60: (60+44)/8 = 13. So nametable col 13, row 11.\n\nHmm wait, but that computation: get_tile_at_pixel probably returns screen tile coords. Let me read nametable region around col 13.\n\nActually, from the first screenshot the ? block at screen (44,88). scroll_x=60. nametable x = (60+44)/8 = 13. Let me read nametable 1 x=12 y=10 width=4 height=4.\n\nAlso for the brick-row ? block at (39,152): tile_x=4, tile_y=19, scroll 60 → nametable col (60+36)/8=12, row 19.\n\nActually easier: search the nametable for tiles $53-$56. Let me just read a wider nametable region. nt width max 32. Let me read nametable 1 x=0 y=0 width=32 height=30? That's a lot. But maybe find the ? block.\n\nActually, the mapping already documented: \"? block: tiles $53/$54 (top half) $55/$56 (bottom half)\". Hmm but the get_tile_at_pixel gave $56 at (44,88) which according to that would be bottom-right. But y=88 should be top half if block spans 88-103... \n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe get_tile_at_pixel returns the nametable tile index directly relative to BG pattern table base and the screen box. For the block at (44,88): tile_id 86 = $56. If the block is at screen x 40-55, y 88-103, then (44,88) is top-left → should be $53. But we got $56. So maybe tile_x=5 isn't the block's left column. Hmm, screen box x=44 means the tile occupies x=44-51 (since tile grid is 8-aligned: 44? no, tiles at multiples of 8: 40-47, 48-55). screen_box x=44 is odd — it means the \"screen_box\" is the actual pixel position maybe accounting for scroll remainder. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me just directly read the nametable raw memory and find the tiles. Better: get_tile_at_pixel with precise=false? Or read_nametable nametable 1 and find $53-$56.\n\nLet me read a big chunk: read_nametable nametable 1 x=0 y=0 width=32 height=30 is too big but fine? It returns 960 entries. That's a lot of output. Let me instead read raw ppu memory nametable. Actually read_nametable returns tile IDs; let me read region around the block.\n\nLet me compute properly. Screen shows ? block around x=38-50. Actually from screenshot, the ? block spans approximately x=37-50, y=82-95. In tile terms x=4.6-6.25. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misreading the screenshot. The ? block center is around x=44, y=88, spanning 16x16: x=36-51, y=80-95. So tile columns 4 and 5 (32-47? no 4*8=32-39, 5=40-47). Hmm 36 isn't tile-aligned on screen because scroll isn't tile-aligned (scroll_x=60, not multiple of 8... 60 = 7.5 tiles). So screen x = nametable_x*8 - scroll_x + 256? Let's see: scroll_x=60. nametable col 13 → 13*8=104, screen x = 104-60 = 44. Yes! So tile at nametable col 13 spans screen x 44-51. And nametable col 12 spans screen x 36-43. So the ? block top-left tile is at nametable col 12, row 11 (screen y 88 since scroll_y=0). And screen_box reported x=44 for tile_x=5... so get_tile_at_pixel's tile_x=5 corresponds to nametable col 13. So tile_x is screen tile column (44/8=5.5→5? Actually 44/8=5.5, floor=5). Hmm, and nametable col 13 = 5 + floor(60/8)=5+7=12? no 5+7=12, not 13. Hmm: 5 + 60/8 = 5+7.5 = 12.5. Not integer.\n\nLet me not overanalyze; let me just read nametable region x=10..16, y=10..13.\n\nActually, the cleanest: read_nametable nametable 1 x=2 y=10 width=16 height=4. That covers nametable cols 2-17 which includes both. Wait but the ? block might be in nametable 1 while the HUD is nametable 0. Let me read nametable 1 cols 10-16 rows 10-13.\n\nHmm, but the earlier read of nametable 1 cols 3-8 rows 10-13 returned all 36 (sky fill). 36 = $24 sky blank. Good, that's consistent (sky). So the ? block is not in cols 3-8. Let me read cols 9-18.\n\nLet me read_nametable nametable 1 x=9 y=10 width=10 height=4. And also the lower block: rows 18-21, cols 9-16.\n\nActually let me just read a big region: nametable 1 x=8 y=8 width=12 height=16 (covers rows 8-23, cols 8-19). That includes both blocks. 192 entries — acceptable.\n\nLet me do that."}
